</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Ablation Study</h3>

<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">How do the layer and timestep affect the foreground mask extraction?</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.2 Comparison with SOTA ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ BachVid: Training-Free Video Generation with Consistent Background and Character" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> presents a visualization of mask extraction across different layers and timesteps.
We observe that aggregating features from all layers often leads to suboptimal results, as certain layers (e.g., 1, 31, 42) introduce noise that interferes with accurate mask extraction. In contrast, aggregating features only from the selected layers <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{mask}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>mask</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{mask}}</annotation></semantics></math> yields a clean and consistent mask.
In addition, mask extraction tends to be unreliable at the early stages of denoising, while performing it too late can also degrade the results.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">How do the layer and timestep affect the matching point identification?</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 4.2 Comparison with SOTA ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ BachVid: Training-Free Video Generation with Consistent Background and Character" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> presents a visualization of point matching identification across different layers and timesteps.
We find that aggregating features from all layers often produces suboptimal results, as certain layers (e.g., 1, 32) introduce interference that disrupts point matching. In contrast, using features only from the selected layer <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{match}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>match</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{match}}</annotation></semantics></math> yields more robust and reliable matching points.
Moreover, point matching is also sensitive to timing: performing it too early or too late in the denoising process is unstable.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">How does the layer affect the KV Injection?</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We also conduct an ablation study on the metrics for selecting vital layers.
DINOv2 similarity measures the similarity between <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{-l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{-l}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Score_{\text{DINOv2}}=\frac{1}{T^{\prime}}\sum_{i=1}^{T^{\prime}}\text{CosSim}(\mathcal{E}(Z[i]),\mathcal{E}(Z^{-l}[i])," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mtext>DINOv2</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mtext>CosSim</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Score_{\text{DINOv2}}=\frac{1}{T^{\prime}}\sum_{i=1}^{T^{\prime}}\text{CosSim}(\mathcal{E}(Z[i]),\mathcal{E}(Z^{-l}[i]),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> is the pre-trained DINOv2 encoder.
So we denote <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{DINOv2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>DINOv2</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{DINOv2}}</annotation></semantics></math> as the 15 vital layers.
We notice that skip layer <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\in\{3,5,6,8,9,10\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\in\{3,5,6,8,9,10\}</annotation></semantics></math> results in low DINOv2 similarity but keeps a high aesthetic score.
This is because the generated videos still keep moderate quality, but show different diversity (see <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math> in Fig. <a href="#S4.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ 4.2 Comparison with SOTA ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ BachVid: Training-Free Video Generation with Consistent Background and Character" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>). Table. <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ How does the layer affect the KV Injection? ‣ 4.3 Ablation Study ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ BachVid: Training-Free Video Generation with Consistent Background and Character" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> quanlitatively demonstrate that injecting KV of <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{aes}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>aes</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{aes}}</annotation></semantics></math> performs better than <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{DINOv2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>DINOv2</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{DINOv2}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span><span id="S4.T2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ablation study on <math id="S4.T2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{kv}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">kv</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{kv}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
<div id="S4.T2.4" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:223.4pt;height:27.9pt;vertical-align:-12.1pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-37.2pt,4.7pt) scale(0.8,0.8) ;">
